    "slug": "secure-your-repository",
    "title": "Secure your repository in 5 minutes",
    "description": "The GitHub settings that turn review into an enforced gate before volunteer work can change your project.",
    "html": "<h1>Secure your repository in 5 minutes</h1>\n<p><strong>A rule that isn&#39;t switched on is a wish.</strong> GitHub enforces what you configure, and nothing else. Volunteers&#39; changes only ever arrive as pull requests, and our server checks them before you see them. These GitHub settings make your own review an <strong>enforced gate</strong>, not a good intention. They protect you from mistakes by anyone: volunteers, staff, or a tool gone wrong. Your account page shows which of the first three are on.</p>\n<h2 id=\"the-controls-and-what-each-one-does\">The controls, and what each one does</h2>\n<div class=\"table-scroll\" tabindex=\"0\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">GitHub setting</th><th scope=\"col\">What it enforces</th><th scope=\"col\">Why it matters with volunteers&#39; AI</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td><strong>Pull requests required</strong></td><td>Every change is proposed first, never written straight into your project.</td><td>You see every change before it counts.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Required reviews</strong></td><td>A person must approve before merging.</td><td>Someone on your side is always accountable.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Required status checks</strong></td><td>Your tests must pass before merging.</td><td>Evidence becomes a rule, not a hope.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>CODEOWNERS</strong></td><td>Changes to chosen folders automatically ask chosen people to review.</td><td>The right expert sees high-risk changes, such as your website&#39;s payment page.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Rulesets / branch protection</strong></td><td>One place that holds these rules for your main branch.</td><td>Nobody can skip them by accident, including admins.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Environments</strong></td><td>Deployments and secrets wait for a named person&#39;s approval.</td><td>Code from a pull request can&#39;t reach your passwords or publish live without a human.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Secret scanning and push protection</strong></td><td>Passwords and keys are detected, and blocked before they&#39;re saved.</td><td>A pasted key never reaches your history.</td></tr>\n<tr><td><strong>Code scanning and Dependabot alerts</strong></td><td>Known security problems in code and libraries are flagged.</td><td>Risky changes get a second, automatic look.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>\n<h2 id=\"do-these-five-about-five-minutes\">Do these five (about five minutes)</h2>\n<h3 id=\"1-protect-your-main-branch\">1. Protect your main branch</h3>\n<p><em>Settings → Rules → Rulesets → New branch ruleset.</em> Target your default branch (usually <code>main</code>) and turn on:</p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Require a pull request before merging</strong>, with <strong>1 required approval</strong>;</li>\n<li><strong>Require status checks to pass</strong>, and choose your test check (run it once first so it appears);</li>\n<li><strong>Block force pushes</strong>, and <strong>Restrict deletions</strong>.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Leave the bypass list empty. Give each check job a unique name, or checks can block each other (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-protected-branches/about-protected-branches\" rel=\"noopener\">about protected branches</a>).</p>\n<p>Private repository? Rulesets there need a paid plan, which <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/nonprofit/nonprofit-teams-plan/getting-started-with-the-github-team-plan-for-nonprofits\" rel=\"noopener\">verified nonprofits get free</a>. See <a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/configuring-branches-and-merges-in-your-repository/managing-rulesets/about-rulesets\" rel=\"noopener\">about rulesets</a>.</p>\n<h3 id=\"2-add-a-codeowners-file\">2. Add a CODEOWNERS file</h3>\n<p>Create <code>.github/CODEOWNERS</code> from our <a href=\"/handbook/templates\">template</a>. At minimum, make yourself the owner of <code>.github/</code> (your automation) and of anything that publishes or handles money or personal data. Then turn on <strong>Require review from Code Owners</strong> in the ruleset.</p>\n<h3 id=\"3-keep-secrets-away-from-pull-requests\">3. Keep secrets away from pull requests</h3>\n<p>Pull requests run your tests with the volunteer&#39;s code, so treat that code as untrusted until reviewed.</p>\n<ul>\n<li>Your test workflow should need <strong>no secrets</strong> and set <code>permissions: contents: read</code> (<a href=\"/handbook/templates\">template</a>).</li>\n<li>Put deployments and anything with a secret in an <strong>environment</strong> with <strong>required reviewers</strong> (<em>Settings → Environments</em>), and only for the main branch.</li>\n<li>Never use <code>pull_request_target</code> to run pull-request code (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/security/securely-using-pull_request_target\" rel=\"noopener\">why</a>). Environment secrets stay locked until a required reviewer approves the job (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/actions/reference/workflows-and-actions/deployments-and-environments\" rel=\"noopener\">environments</a>).</li>\n<li>Our app&#39;s pull requests come from branches in your own repository, so their workflows start without an &quot;Approve and run workflows&quot; step. That&#39;s why your pull-request workflows must not use secrets.</li>\n</ul>\n<h3 id=\"4-turn-on-security-alerts\">4. Turn on security alerts</h3>\n<p><em>Settings → Code security</em>: turn on <strong>Dependabot alerts</strong>, <strong>Secret scanning</strong> and <strong>Push protection</strong>, and for code projects <strong>Code scanning (default setup)</strong>. These are free for public repositories. Push protection isn&#39;t on by default, even there (<a href=\"https://docs.github.com/en/repositories/managing-your-repositorys-settings-and-features/enabling-features-for-your-repository/managing-security-and-analysis-settings-for-your-repository\" rel=\"noopener\">security settings</a>).</p>\n<h3 id=\"5-two-factor-sign-in-for-everyone\">5. Two-factor sign-in for everyone</h3>\n<p><em>Organization settings → Authentication security → Require two-factor authentication.</em></p>\n<h2 id=\"what-our-side-adds\">What our side adds</h2>\n<p>Before a pull request reaches you, our server has already:</p>\n<ul>\n<li>made the volunteer&#39;s AI state a plan, and kept its edits to the files in your task;</li>\n<li>refused changes with hidden text or secrets, and listed other warning signs (new network calls, code that runs commands, install scripts, long encoded strings);</li>\n<li>run your checks on our test machine, where your task has them;</li>\n<li>written the review checklist into the pull request.</li>\n</ul>\n<p>Our GitHub App can only create branches and open pull requests. It can&#39;t merge, can&#39;t change settings, and refuses changes to <code>.github/</code>.</p>\n<h2 id=\"if-something-gets-through\">If something gets through</h2>\n<p>Close the pull request. If it was already merged, use GitHub&#39;s <strong>Revert</strong> button on the pull request, then tell us. We pause the task and look at the volunteer&#39;s record. See <a href=\"/handbook/what-happens-when\">What happens when…</a>.</p>\n",
